Most applications use `driver.Session` and a transport. The modules below
matter when you need to host many sessions, supply an event loop, add a codec,
or drive the protocol without I/O. The diagram above is the map, and
[architecture](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/docs/architecture.md)
is the full account.

# Choose the right level

| Name | Job |
| --- | --- |
| **Peer** | The caller-owned engine handle. You feed inbound frames, call `step`, drain outbound frames, and read events. It opens no sockets. |
| **Session** | The one-partner application object: `connect` / `listen`, `fetch`, blocking `call`. It is a view onto a conversation its Host owns. |
| **Server** | The listening side. It publishes objects, and each `accept` returns a `Session` for one partner that connected. |
| **HostRuntime** | The loop that runs `Session` and `Server`. It keeps one identity across several socket-backed sessions over the transports you register. |
| **HostTurn** | The argument an object's `handle` gets. It carries the turn's arena for building replies and the Host for calling other objects. |
| **Host** | The multi-session CapTP product: one identity, many sessions. It routes introductions between sessions and settles promise waits (Goblins calls a promise a *vow*). Its socket-free core lives in `driver/core`. |
| **Bridge** | The Zig embedder that answers the Host's action queue (dial, flush, close, entropy) over a completion loop. Native bindings use the driver archive for that loop. The browser package drives the Host itself. |

Use `Session` unless you need one of the lower-level responsibilities in the
table. An introduction settles as a live capability because every `Session`
is backed by a Host. Use `Bridge` with a completion loop when one thread must
serve many connections. [Concepts]($link.page('concepts')) covers three-party
introductions.

# The protocol core

The core is synchronous. It holds no sockets and starts no background work, so
you can test it without a host runtime.

- **foundation** owns shared primitives: a `Frame` owns bytes, a `FrameView`
  borrows them, and IDs are plain values.
- **model** is the CapTP vocabulary, independent of the bytes that carried it.
- **codec** turns model values into wire bytes and back.
  [Syrup](https://github.com/ocapn/syrup) is what Goblins speaks.
  [Cap'n Proto](https://capnproto.org/) is the second encoding.
  [Codecs]($link.page('codecs')) compares them.
- **session** is the authority: import, export, and answer tables, and what is
  valid. A codec never decides whether a reference is allowed.
- **engine** is the `Peer` above. It only dispatches.

# Above the core

- **actors** is a Goblins-shaped `Vat`: near-call `$`, eventual-send `<-`, and
  promises. It adds no protocol semantics the engine lacks.
- **driver/core** is the multi-session serve core with no sockets of its own.
  A build check keeps I/O out of it.
- **net** is where `std.Io` enters. Residents include
  [iroh](https://www.iroh.computer/) QUIC, `noise-tcp`,
  [WebRTC]($link.page('transports/webrtc')),
  [onion]($link.page('transports/onion')), then cleartext `tcp`,
  `keyed-tcp`, and `inproc`, among others
  ([Transports]($link.page('transports'))).
- **loam** stores objects, and the capabilities they hold, across restarts.
  Its core does no I/O. An optional LMDB module supplies the disk.
  [Loam]($link.page('loam')) explains records, sleepy actors, and durable
  names.

> **Vat**: one single-threaded container of objects, the unit
> [Spritely Goblins](https://spritely.institute/goblins/) and the wider ocap
> world organize around. Every call into a vat runs as one *turn*, start to
> finish, before the next begins.

> **Near-call (`$`) and eventual-send (`<-`)**: from
> [E](https://web.archive.org/web/20241231230559/http://erights.org/). Near runs now in your vat. Eventual queues a later
> turn and returns a promise. That is also the only way to call across a
> session.

# Why Zig

Memory control. An allocator is an argument in Zig, so nothing allocates
without one being passed in, and there is no collector deciding when to run.
[Performance]($link.page('performance')) publishes what glowcap costs.

The compiler does not prove memory safety in exchange, so glowcap owes that in
tests: leak checks, allocation budgets, and fuzzing on untrusted input.

# Public entry points

[Choose an API]($link.page('surfaces')) compares the entry points available to
applications.

- **c_abi** is opaque handles, fixed-width codes, `ptr + len`, and explicit
  release. `libglowcap_host` is the CapTP host the bindings wrap. Native
  bindings also load `libglowcap_driver` to run the loop.
- **driver** is native Zig above `driver/core`: `Session` and `HostRuntime` for
  ordinary native use, plus `Bridge` when one readiness loop must carry many
  connections.

[Bindings]($link.page('bindings')) provide the application API in Rust,
TypeScript, Python, and Racket, each over the C ABI.
